, 0.0311279296875, 0.0419921875, -0.00490570068359375, -0.0190582275390625, -0.0120849609375, 0.01690673828125, 0.005401611328125, -0.0158843994140625, 0.0445556640625, -0.0271453857421875, 0.0275726318359375, 0.0120697021484375, 0.0169830322265625, 0.0254364013671875, 0.01218414306640625, 0.039642333984375, -0.006683349609375, 0.044769287109375, 0.00908660888671875, 0.03277587890625, -0.01239013671875, -0.0006651878356933594, -0.003940582275390625, -0.0237274169921875, 0.003170013427734375, -0.0007452964782714844, -0.02239990234375, 0.0176849365234375, 0.05267333984375, -0.0185089111328125, -0.016265869140625, -0.018707275390625, 0.0283660888671875, -0.0182952880859375, -0.01442718505859375, 0.0435791015625, 0.0193328857421875, -0.00806427001953125, -0.034423828125, -0.0032711029052734375, -0.01169586181640625, -0.01204681396484375, -0.036285400390625, 0.012176513671875, -0.01459503173828125, -0.01149749755859375, 0.0023193359375, -0.0172882080078125, 0.03448486328125, 0.0096893310546875, -0.0019016265869140625, -0.01096343994140625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Large-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, most existing pipelines focus on optimizing task-specific datasets in isolation, which limits cross-capability transfer and systematic organization. A core challenge remains: effectively curating diverse datasets while organizing various sources of supervision based on dependencies among generative capabilities.\n\nIn this work, we introduce a **capability-driven data infrastructure**\u2014a unified framework for large-scale multimodal model training. This infrastructure rests on two key concepts: (1) **capability-specific supervision construction**, the targeted assembly of datasets tailored to distinct generative abilities, and (2) **capability-aligned curriculum scheduling**, where training data and tasks are sequenced according to the logical progression of learning increasingly complex capabilities.\n\nOur framework employs three interoperable **data engines**:\n- **Text-image grounding** constructs direct associations between natural language and visual content, such as pairing descriptive captions with corresponding images to enable text-to-image generation.\n- **Inter-image transformation** captures transformations between related images (e.g., edits or style changes), facilitating tasks like image editing and concept transfer.\n- **Image-knowledge association** links images to structured or factual knowledge entities (such as object categories or named entities), enhancing semantic grounding and knowledge-based generation.\n\nTo ensure consistency and task alignment, **caption experts**\u2014automated or human annotators specialized in linguistic quality\u2014refine and harmonize text representations across tasks and levels of granularity, bridging T2I (text-to-image) generation and editing scenarios.\n\nWe jointly evolve the training curriculum to align with capability dependencies: the sequence of tasks and data is shaped by the natural order in which models acquire generative skills, such as learning to synthesize basic scenes before mastering fine-grained edits. Throughout, a **capability-aware evaluation** system employs targeted retrieval benchmarks, expert annotation for specific failure cases, and gap-aware resampling to iteratively improve data quality.\n\nAt scale, this approach collects and curates a 440M-image T2I corpus, 120M editing pairs, and over 27M image-entity pairs\u2014exceeding the scale of widely used benchmarks like LAION-5B and improving coverage of editing and entity association tasks. Leveraging this data, we train scratch-built multimodal diffusion models with 3B and 6B parameters.\n\nFor evaluation, we report both quantitative and qualitative results. On **CPI-Bench**\u2014a comprehensive benchmark assessing compositionality, photorealism, image editing, and entity grounding\u2014we use metrics such as CLIP-based retrieval, FID scores for realism, and precision/recall for entity coverage. Compared to recent models, our systems demonstrate improved compositional accuracy (by +5.2 CIDEr on complex prompts), a broader spectrum of renderable concepts (covering 95% of benchmarked entities), and successful transfer of editing capabilities (reaching 84% accuracy on targeted transformations). Qualitative results further illustrate rich visual coverage across scenarios and reliable generalization between generation and editing tasks.\n\nThese findings underscore the value of a capability-driven approach to data and curriculum design for advancing large-scale multimodal image generation."}}